<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">

  <!-- TBC capture formats and their metadata sidecars.
       Installed to ~/.local/share/mime/packages/ by ~/.local/bin/tbc-desktop-sync.

       This file supersedes the earlier one-type-per-file set
       (application-video-tbc-chroma.xml, application-x-tbc-luma.xml,
       application-x-tbc-chroma.xml) and additionally defines the base
       application/video-tbc type, which until now existed ONLY in
       /usr/share/mime/packages/application-video-tbc.xml — a root-owned file
       belonging to no RPM, left behind by an old `sudo make install`. Defining
       it here means .tbc identification no longer depends on untracked cruft.

       Two file-naming conventions are in play:
         ld-decode / vhs-decode  composite .tbc, chroma companion *_chroma.tbc
         decode-orc              luma .tbcy + chroma .tbcc, sharing one .tbc.db
       .ytbc / .ctbc are legacy aliases that only ld-analyse still recognises;
       they carry the same meaning as .tbcy / .tbcc, so they are extra globs on
       the same types rather than types of their own.

       Every type here is openable by ld-analyse (see isSupportedInputExtension
       in src/ld-analyse/mainwindow.cpp), which is what ld-analyse.desktop
       declares in its MimeType= line. -->


  <!-- GLOB WEIGHTS - why the numbers matter.

       The matcher keeps two lists.  A glob that is a SIMPLE SUFFIX (a single
       leading "*" followed by literal text) goes into a fast suffix trie; any
       pattern with an interior wildcard goes into a slow "full glob" list that
       is consulted ONLY when the trie finds nothing.  Weight breaks ties inside
       the trie, longest-match breaks ties at equal weight.

       Every glob below is a simple suffix, so they all live in the trie and
       weight is the whole story:

         *_chroma.tbc  needs w60 to beat *.tbc  (both match "foo_chroma.tbc")
         *.tbc.db      needs w80 to beat *.json / *.db style competition
         *.tbc.json    needs w80 to beat *.json (w50, from freedesktop.org.xml)
         *.tbcy/.tbcc/.ytbc/.ctbc  have no competitors, default weight is fine

       NOT REPRESENTABLE: vhs-decode's prefix form "chroma_*.tbc".  That pattern
       has an interior wildcard, so it lands in the full-glob list, which is
       never reached because *.tbc always matches in the trie first.  No weight
       can fix this.  Those files stay application/video-tbc, which is harmless:
       ld-analyse detects the chroma_ prefix itself when it opens them. -->


  <!-- MAGIC vs GLOB - why the metadata types need sub-class-of.

       When a magic (content-sniffing) rule matches with priority < 80, the
       filename-derived type is kept only if it is a SUBCLASS of the sniffed
       type; otherwise the sniffed type wins outright.  Weight does not enter
       into it.

       .tbc.db is a SQLite database, and SQLite's "SQLite format 3" magic sits
       at the default priority 50.  Without <sub-class-of application/vnd.sqlite3>
       every .tbc.db would resolve to application/vnd.sqlite3 and open in
       sqlitebrowser no matter what glob weight we gave it.

       .tbc.json has no such problem (application/json carries no magic rule, and
       our w80 glob beats its w50 *.json), but it is subclassed anyway so that
       text editors registered for JSON stay available under "Open With". -->


  <!-- GENERIC-ICON - why the metadata types repeat their own icon name.

       GTK resolves a themed-icon name list by iterating THEMES in the outer loop
       and names in the inner loop.  A conventional fallback such as
       <generic-icon name="application-x-sqlite3"/> would be found inside Adwaita
       on the very first theme, and the lookup would stop there — our icon in
       hicolor is never reached, however early it appears in the name list.

       Repeating the specific name means no name in the list exists in Adwaita,
       so the lookup falls through Adwaita -> AdwaitaLegacy -> hicolor and finds
       ours.  Verify with:  gio info FILE | grep standard::icon
       then look the names up in order against the active theme.

       Only the two metadata types need this: they subclass types Adwaita has
       icons for.  The four video types below subclass application/video-tbc,
       which Adwaita has never heard of, so there is nothing to lose to. -->


  <mime-type type="application/video-tbc">
    <comment>CVBS video file</comment>
    <icon name="application-video-tbc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tbc"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/video-tbc-chroma">
    <comment>Chroma TBC video file</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/video-tbc"/>
    <icon name="application-video-tbc-chroma"/>
    <glob pattern="*_chroma.tbc" weight="60"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x-tbc-luma">
    <comment>TBC luma (Y) video</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/video-tbc"/>
    <icon name="application-x-tbc-luma"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tbcy"/>
    <glob pattern="*.ytbc"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x-tbc-chroma">
    <comment>TBC chroma (C) video</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/video-tbc"/>
    <icon name="application-x-tbc-chroma"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tbcc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.ctbc"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x-tbc-metadata">
    <comment>TBC metadata database</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/vnd.sqlite3"/>
    <icon name="application-x-tbc-metadata"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x-tbc-metadata"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tbc.db" weight="80"/>
  </mime-type>

  <mime-type type="application/x-tbc-metadata-json">
    <comment>TBC metadata (legacy JSON)</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/json"/>
    <icon name="application-x-tbc-metadata-json"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x-tbc-metadata-json"/>
    <glob pattern="*.tbc.json" weight="80"/>
  </mime-type>

</mime-info>
